Both issues are already in the ZK, and they're **siblings**: one phenomenon (`:label` references) breaking at two different pipeline layers. Both are tagged `bug`/`upstream`/`deferred`, both were surfaced by the GRAM label-resolution work (PR #9), and both sit on the **Row Types** + **Pipeline Stabilization** threads (issue #2 also on **Verification Backend**). Neither has a plan yet. I grounded the zettels against the code and found the mechanisms are more precise than the notes say — details below, including drift you'll want to fix.

---

## Issue #1 — sigma-annotated struct loses sibling-label scope

**Zettel:** [[checking-path-label-unbound]] (titled "Checking-path label scope loss"). **Status: live.** The repro is a shipped snapshot test that captures the failure as an error:

```13:18:src/__tests__/integration/records.test.ts
	test("self-referencing fields", () => {
		const result = runScript(`
let rectangle: { width: Num, height: Num, area: Num }
    = { width: 10, height: 20, area: :width * :height };
		`);
```
```695:702:src/__tests__/integration/__snapshots__/records.test.ts.snap
exports[`Language Tour — Records & Tuples > self-referencing fields 1`] = `
[
  {
    "error": "Unbound label: width",
```

### Precise root cause (sharper than the zettel)
The throw is at **NbE evaluation time**, not elaboration lookup:

```100:118:src/elaboration/normalization/evaluation.v2.ts
		.with({ type: "Var", variable: { type: "Label" } }, ({ variable }) => {
			const sig = ctx.sigma[variable.name];
			if (sig) { globalResultStack.push(sig.value); return; }
			const rec = ctx.record[variable.name];
			...
			throw new Error("Unbound label: " + variable.name);
		})
```

The `[struct, Sigma]` check case does infer → **evaluate** → apply-closure → recheck:

```140:153:src/elaboration/check.ts
				.with([{ type: "struct" }, NF.Patterns.Sigma], ([tm, sig]) =>
					V2.Do(function* () {
						const [rtm] = yield* EB.infer.gen(tm);
						const rv = NF.evaluate(ctx, rtm);
						assert(rv.type === "App" && rv.arg.type === "Row", ...);
						const ty = NF.apply(sig.binder, sig.closure, NF.Constructors.Row(rv.arg.row));
						return yield* Check.val.gen(tm, ty);
					}),
				)
```

Step 1 (`infer`) runs inside `withLabelContext`, which populates `ctx.labels` **and** `ctx.sigma` — but only in a `V2.local` scope that's popped when inference returns:

```37:50:src/elaboration/inference/rows.ts
export const withLabelContext = <A>(row: Src.Row, f: V2.Elaboration<A>): V2.Elaboration<A> =>
	V2.Do(function* () {
		...
		return yield* V2.local(
			ctx_ =>
				entries(bindings).reduce((ctx, [label, type]) => {
					const withLabel = EB.extendLabel(ctx, label, type);
					const neutral = NF.Constructors.Neutral(NF.Constructors.Var({ type: "Label", name: label }));
					return { ...withLabel, sigma: { ...withLabel.sigma, [label]: { value: neutral } } };
				}, ctx_),
			f,
		);
	});
```

Step 2's `NF.evaluate(ctx, rtm)` then runs in the **outer** `ctx`, which no longer holds those sibling `sigma` bindings, so evaluating the `area: :width * :height` field throws. The sibling `[struct, Schema]` case (check.ts:129-138) works because it checks field-by-field inside a `V2.local` extend-label scope and never evaluates a label-bearing term bare.

So the zettel's "resolves against an empty label context and is reported unbound" is directionally right but wrong on the where: the context **is** established (transiently, during inference); the failure is the subsequent bare `NF.evaluate` in the Sigma path. Resolution is via `ctx.sigma`/`ctx.record` at eval time — not `ctx.labels` at lookup time.

### Clarifying your framing
Your description ("annotated with sigma types — record types with `:label`") conflates the two mechanisms that [[sigma-vs-codata-label-refs]] explicitly separates:

- **`:label` in the type** (`{ fst: Num, snd: :fst }`) — the **sigma / dependent** mechanism. This **works** (see the passing `dependent-records.test.ts` singleton cases; fixed by [[sigma-checking-infer-constrain]] and [[sigma-quoting-field-ref]]).
- **`:label` in the value** (`{ …, area: :width }`) — the **codata / eager self-reference** mechanism ([[recursive-struct-binding]]). This is what's **broken under annotation**. Note the failing repro's annotation is a *plain* record type (`{ width: Num, … }`, no `:label`); it breaks because plain record annotations elaborate to `Σ(...) . Schema [...]` (confirmed by the "records" snapshot), so a self-referencing value struct checked against *any* record type takes the `[struct, Sigma]` path. Unannotated, it works end-to-end (inference never evaluates the struct; GRAM's knot-tying resolves the eager refs later).

### Intent / semantics
`area: :width * :height` should read the concrete sibling **values** (10, 20 → 200) — the value-level fixed-point ([[recursive-struct-binding]], [[sigma-value-semantics]]). The fix is upstream of GRAM: the checking/Sigma path must re-establish the same sibling sigma bindings inference sets up *before* it evaluates the fields (or avoid the full `NF.evaluate` — the `TODO:QUESTION` at check.ts:144 already gestures at this). Two further design notes: `ctx.sigma` is a **flat map** that needs to become a **stack** for nested dependent records ([[sigma-types]], [[sigma-bindings]]); and there's an open, `needs-design` fork on the recursion thread — [[design-sigma-codata-label-refs]] — over whether value-level self-reference should get its own **ν-binder core** (`ν self. …`, `:width` → `self.width`) instead of sharing `:label` with type-level sigma. That fork could change the core representation the fix targets, so it's worth a decision before implementing.

